(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-case-vignettes"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Case Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), and two separate blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. A transthoracic echocardiogram shows no vegetation. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Transesophageal echocardiography","Treat for 1 week, then reassess","Stop the workup; echo was normal","Repeat transthoracic study in 6 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is highly sensitive and specific, detects vegetations and defines the extent of disease, and it is required for optimal assessment of possible prosthetic valve endocarditis. A clear transthoracic study does not settle the question in a prosthetic valve with Staphylococcus aureus in the blood. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 25"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man completed 4 weeks of ceftriaxone for Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) endocarditis. A colonoscopy last week was normal. Which other condition is this organism associated with?","choices":["Urinary tract infection","Dental infection","Liver disease or cirrhosis","Injection drug use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) is associated with gastrointestinal abnormalities, especially colon cancer, and with liver disease or cirrhosis. With a normal colonoscopy, liver disease is the other association to keep in mind. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 17, 18"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman is seen in the office with 3 weeks of low-grade fever, weight loss, a new murmur and painful red nodules on several finger pads. Her vital signs are stable. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Admit for cultures and intravenous therapy","Oral amoxicillin, recheck in 1 week","Outpatient echo next week, no labs","One intramuscular ceftriaxone dose now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Suspected endocarditis is managed in hospital: blood cultures first, then intravenous antibiotics, hospital admission and close monitoring of labs, renal function and cardiovascular status by a multidisciplinary team. Stable vital signs do not make it an office problem. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 25, 26"},{"q":"Four patients are on day 5 of appropriate intravenous antibiotics for native valve endocarditis. Which one needs evaluation for surgery?","choices":["Fever resolved, cultures now negative","Mild anemia with a normal cell size","New heart failure from severe regurgitation","Splinter hemorrhages still visible"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure due to valvular dysfunction is the most common indication for surgery, alongside infection not resolving on appropriate antibiotics, abscess and fungal infection. New heart failure from severe regurgitation calls for cardiothoracic surgery evaluation. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 12, 25, 33"},{"q":"A patient with which of the following conditions requires antimicrobial prophylaxis before dental surgery?","choices":["Atrial septal defect","Previous coronary artery bypass grafting","Mitral valve prolapse, no regurgitation","Previous infective endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prophylaxis is reserved for high-risk cardiac conditions: prosthetic valves or valve-repair material, previous infective endocarditis, certain congenital heart disease and cardiac transplant with valve regurgitation. A past episode of endocarditis is on that list, so dental work that manipulates the gums or tooth roots needs prophylaxis. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 5; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 36"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with a history of injection drug use is evaluated in the emergency department for fever and fatigue. He is cachectic and diaphoretic. His pulse is regular at 110 beats per minute, temperature 38.4 °C (101.1 °F) orally and blood pressure 128/68 mm Hg (right arm, seated). A high-pitched murmur is heard along the left lower sternal border, and small painless hemorrhages are noted on the conjunctivae, palate and palms. What is the recommended initial treatment?","choices":["Intramuscular penicillin, one dose","Follow lactic acid, start antibiotics if above 2.5 mmol/L","Begin an aminoglycoside with penicillin and cefepime","Begin vancomycin, adjust per blood culture results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever, tachycardia, a new left lower sternal border murmur and painless hemorrhages on the conjunctivae, palate and palms in a person who injects drugs point to endocarditis, most often from Staphylococcus aureus, which may be methicillin-resistant. Vancomycin begins once blood cultures are drawn and is adjusted when the organism and its sensitivities return. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 2; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 26, 27"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve is scheduled for bronchoscopy, during which a biopsy of the airway mucosa is planned. He has no drug allergies and takes pills. Which plan fits?","choices":["No prophylaxis for bronchoscopy","Oral amoxicillin beforehand","Oral clindamycin beforehand","Amoxicillin for 3 days after"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A prosthetic valve is high risk, and invasive respiratory procedures that incise or biopsy the respiratory mucosa call for prophylaxis. Bronchoscopy is exempt only when no mucosal incision such as a biopsy is made; his includes one, so he takes a single oral amoxicillin dose 30-60 minutes before. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 35"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man was recently diagnosed with colon adenocarcinoma. He now has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and a new murmur, and transesophageal echocardiography shows a mitral valve vegetation. He has had no dental work, urinary symptoms or drug use. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Enterococcus faecalis","Streptococcus gallolyticus","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) comes from the gastrointestinal tract and is associated with gastrointestinal abnormalities, especially colon cancer, and with liver disease. His new colon cancer, with no dental, urinary or injection source, points to it. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has 10 days of fever to 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) and fatigue that began after a dental procedure. A new holosystolic murmur of mitral regurgitation is heard, and she has thin dark lines under several fingernails and pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctivae. Transesophageal echocardiography shows a vegetation on the mitral valve, and two separate blood cultures grow viridans streptococci. What is her modified Duke classification?","choices":["Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: two major criteria","Possible: three minor criteria","Definite: five minor criteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococci, a typical organism, in two separate blood cultures is one major criterion, and a vegetation on transesophageal echocardiography is the second. Two major criteria make endocarditis definite without counting minor ones; her fever and conjunctival hemorrhages are minor criteria on top. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 24"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man who injects drugs took 10 days of oral antibiotics from an urgent care clinic for a chest cold before any blood cultures were drawn. He now has a temperature of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Three sets of blood cultures show no growth, and transesophageal echocardiography shows an oscillating mass on the tricuspid valve. He has no skin, eye or kidney findings. What is his modified Duke classification?","choices":["Definite: two major criteria","Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: three minor criteria","Possible: one major, two minor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The oscillating mass is a major criterion. Injection drug use and fever are two minor criteria. His cultures are negative because antibiotics were given before they were drawn, so there is no second major criterion. One major plus two minor meets possible (one major and one minor) but not definite. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman who had rheumatic heart disease as a teenager and now has mitral regurgitation has a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F) and painful, red nodules on several finger pads. Blood cultures are pending, and transesophageal echocardiography shows an oscillating mass on the mitral valve. What is her modified Duke classification?","choices":["Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, two minor","Definite: two major criteria","Possible: three minor criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The oscillating mass on echocardiography is a major criterion (endocardial involvement). Her rheumatic mitral regurgitation (predisposition), fever of 38.6 °C and painful finger-pad nodules (an immunologic phenomenon) are three minor criteria. One major plus three minor is definite endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral regurgitation has 6 weeks of low-grade fever, night sweats and weight loss. Transesophageal echocardiography shows a mitral vegetation, and three sets of blood cultures grow Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, a HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism. She had anaphylaxis to penicillin. Which antibiotic class could be considered?","choices":["Ampicillin-sulbactam","Vancomycin plus gentamicin","A fluoroquinolone","Nafcillin or oxacillin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) endocarditis is treated with ceftriaxone or another third-generation cephalosporin, and for a patient with a penicillin allergy a fluoroquinolone could be considered. Her anaphylaxis to penicillin is the comorbidity that points to this class. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 31"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve is intubated in intensive care and needs an urgent dental extraction. She has no drug allergies and cannot take anything by mouth. Which prophylaxis fits?","choices":["Intravenous ceftriaxone","Oral amoxicillin","Intravenous clindamycin","Oral azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A prosthetic valve is a high-risk condition, and an extraction manipulates the gums. A patient with no penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication gets an injection: ampicillin, or cefazolin or ceftriaxone, intramuscular or intravenous, 30-60 minutes before the procedure. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man who was treated for infective endocarditis 4 years ago has 3 weeks of fever to 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) and painful red nodules on two finger pads. Two separate blood cultures grow Haemophilus species, a HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism. Transesophageal echocardiography shows no vegetation, abscess or new regurgitation. What is his modified Duke classification?","choices":["Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, two minor","Possible: three minor criteria","Definite: two major criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism, a typical organism, in two separate cultures is a major criterion. Prior endocarditis (predisposition), fever and painful finger-pad nodules (immunologic) are three minor criteria. One major plus three minor is definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman had her mitral valve replaced with a bioprosthesis 3 years ago. She now has 2 weeks of fever to 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), fatigue and a new regurgitant murmur. Three sets of blood cultures have been drawn, and results are pending. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin, gentamicin and cefepime","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Ceftriaxone alone","Nafcillin plus gentamicin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci. Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone is the regimen for native valve endocarditis, a subacute presentation, or a prosthetic valve in place MORE than 1 year, and her bioprosthesis is 3 years old. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 6, 27"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve placed 6 years ago has Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis of the prosthesis and was started on vancomycin, rifampin and gentamicin. Susceptibility testing now shows methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, and he has no drug allergies. Which change is appropriate?","choices":["Stop rifampin and gentamicin","Replace vancomycin with daptomycin","Switch to oral rifampin alone","Replace vancomycin with nafcillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus on a prosthetic valve is treated with an antistaphylococcal drug plus rifampin plus gentamicin. Vancomycin is the drug for methicillin-resistant strains; when cultures show a methicillin-susceptible strain, vancomycin can be changed to nafcillin or oxacillin while rifampin and gentamicin continue. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 28, 30"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl with repaired congenital heart disease has 3 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue and joint aches, and blood cultures grow viridans streptococci. Her parent asks why the doctor found no spots on her hands, nails or eyes. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Children show them more than adults","They appear only with Candida","Those signs are less common in children","Their absence rules out endocarditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Classic peripheral stigmata (Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, Roth spots, splinter hemorrhages) are considerably less common in children than in adults. A child with congenital heart disease usually has an indolent course, prolonged low-grade fever with fatigue and joint aches, as she does. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman who injects drugs was started on vancomycin plus ceftriaxone after three sets of blood cultures were drawn for suspected native tricuspid valve endocarditis. The cultures now grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which adjustment fits?","choices":["Switch to nafcillin","Continue vancomycin alone","Add rifampin and gentamicin","Switch to ceftriaxone alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is adjusted to the culture result. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus on a native valve is treated with vancomycin or daptomycin, so vancomycin continues and ceftriaxone, there to cover streptococci until the organism was known, is no longer needed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 30"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with chronic kidney disease (creatinine clearance 25 mL/min) and recurrent urinary tract infections has 4 weeks of low-grade fever and a new murmur. Transesophageal echocardiography shows a native aortic valve vegetation, and three sets of blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. Which regimen is preferred?","choices":["Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Ceftriaxone alone","Ampicillin plus gentamicin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Enterococcal endocarditis is treated with penicillin or ampicillin plus gentamicin, or with ampicillin plus ceftriaxone, and ampicillin plus ceftriaxone is the combination marked as good for chronic kidney disease or acute kidney injury with a low creatinine clearance. His clearance of 25 mL/min makes the gentamicin-free pairing the better choice. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Four adults are booked for periodontal surgery that will manipulate the gums. Which patient needs endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Pacemaker without valve disease","Coronary artery bypass grafting","Unrepaired cyanotic congenital defect","Native mitral regurgitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease is on the high-risk list, along with prosthetic valves or repair material, previous endocarditis, repaired disease with a residual defect at a prosthetic patch, and transplant valvulopathy. Periodontal surgery manipulates the gums, so prophylaxis is given. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 36"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a bioprosthetic mitral valve placed 4 years ago took a week of oral antibiotics for presumed bronchitis before her fevers were investigated. Three sets of blood cultures now show no growth, but transesophageal echocardiography shows a new vegetation on the prosthesis. Which best explains the negative cultures?","choices":["Antibiotics given before cultures","An atrial myxoma, not infection","Too few culture sets drawn","Endocarditis has been excluded"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Culture-negative endocarditis may be due to organisms not recovered by routine microbiology testing or to antimicrobials given before blood cultures were drawn. Her week of antibiotics before any cultures is the clear explanation, and the vegetation keeps endocarditis on the table. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 21, 25"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man who injects drugs has a temperature of 39.2 °C (102.6 °F), painless, flat hemorrhagic spots on his soles, hemorrhages on both conjunctivae and septic pulmonary infarcts on chest imaging. Blood cultures and echocardiography are pending. How many minor modified Duke criteria does he meet?","choices":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Injection drug use (predisposition) and fever are two minor criteria. Janeway lesions, conjunctival hemorrhages and septic pulmonary infarcts all belong to ONE criterion, vascular phenomena, so together they count once. Three minor criteria make the case possible. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease (creatinine 2.7 mg/dL, normal 0.6-1.2) has uncomplicated native mitral valve endocarditis from penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci and is responding quickly to therapy. Which regimen fits?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus gentamicin, 2 weeks","Daptomycin alone","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Ceftriaxone alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococci are treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone, with or without gentamicin, or with vancomycin. The 2-week regimen that adds gentamicin is only for uncomplicated disease with a rapid response AND no chronic kidney disease, so with a creatinine of 2.7 mg/dL she gets ceftriaxone without gentamicin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 29"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve needs a dental extraction. He had anaphylaxis to amoxicillin 10 years ago and swallows pills without difficulty. Which prophylactic agent fits?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral clindamycin","Oral doxycycline","Oral cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A penicillin-allergic patient who can take oral medication gets azithromycin or clarithromycin, cephalexin or doxycycline, as a single dose 30-60 minutes before the procedure. Cephalexin is ruled out by his anaphylaxis, so doxycycline fits. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with 6 weeks of subacute endocarditis suddenly develops a severe headache and left-sided weakness. Brain imaging shows an intracranial hemorrhage. Which complication best explains this?","choices":["Septic pulmonary emboli","Perivalvular abscess in the septum","Immune complex glomerulonephritis","Ruptured mycotic (infected) aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Subacute endocarditis is gradually progressive unless it is complicated by a major embolic event or a ruptured mycotic (infected) aneurysm, and infective intracranial aneurysms and intracranial hemorrhage are both on the brain's list of complications. A sudden bleed fits a ruptured aneurysm. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 11, 15, 25"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve is having orthodontic brackets placed and will return monthly for adjustments. She has no allergies. Which prophylaxis plan fits?","choices":["Azithromycin before each visit","Amoxicillin before placement only","Amoxicillin before each visit","None for brackets or adjustments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her mechanical valve makes her high risk, but placing orthodontic brackets and adjusting orthodontic appliances are on the list of procedures that do NOT need endocarditis prophylaxis. Prophylaxis is for work that manipulates the gums or tooth roots or perforates the oral mucosa. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 36"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with an isolated atrial septal defect has a temperature of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) and fatigue. One of three blood cultures grows Staphylococcus aureus. Echocardiography shows no vegetation, abscess or new regurgitation, and she has no skin, eye or kidney findings. How many minor modified Duke criteria does she meet?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fever of 38.7 °C is one minor criterion, and a single positive culture that does not meet the major criterion is a second (microbiologic evidence). An isolated atrial septal defect is specifically excluded from the predisposing congenital heart conditions, so it adds nothing. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man who injects drugs has native tricuspid valve endocarditis, and three sets of blood cultures grow methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Vancomycin was stopped on day 2 because of a severe reaction to it. Which antibiotic should replace it?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Daptomycin","Nafcillin","Cefazolin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus on a native valve is treated with vancomycin or daptomycin. With vancomycin stopped because of a severe reaction, daptomycin is the listed alternative for a resistant strain. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 30"},{"q":"A 13-year-old boy with a prosthetic pulmonary valve needs incision and drainage of an infected skin abscess on his forearm. Which is appropriate regarding endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["No prophylaxis; it is not dental","Prophylaxis only if he has a fever","Give prophylaxis before the procedure","Prophylaxis only after drainage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A prosthetic valve is a high-risk pediatric condition, and prophylaxis is given not only for gum-manipulating dental work and invasive respiratory procedures but also when a procedure is performed over infected skin or musculoskeletal tissue. Draining an infected abscess qualifies. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man who injects drugs has a temperature of 39.1 °C (102.4 °F) and painless, flat hemorrhagic spots on his soles. He has no known heart disease. Blood cultures and echocardiography are pending. Which single new result would make his endocarditis definite?","choices":["Two separate cultures with Staphylococcus aureus","Leukocytosis on the blood count","One culture with coagulase-negative staphylococci","A positive rheumatoid factor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Injection drug use, fever and painless flat lesions on the soles (Janeway lesions, a vascular phenomenon) are three minor criteria. Staphylococcus aureus, a typical organism, in two separate blood cultures adds a major criterion, and one major plus three minor is definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 13, 22, 25"}]};
